electromagnet-build-question = We wind { $turns } turns of insulated wire on a steel nail and connect the coil into a circuit with a source, a key, a rheostat and an ammeter. The coil lies with its axis east–west, and on its axis, 15 cm from its middle, stands a compass: with no current its needle looks north — the Earth’s field holds it there ({ $name }{ $value2 }: its horizontal part ≈ { $value } µT). We let 0.5, 1 and 1.5 A through and write down how many degrees the needle turned. Then we reverse the current, and at last pull the nail out. What makes an electromagnet stronger?
